ernal/install 609.117s vs non-race 228.344s = x2.67; atomicity 1422.407s vs 353.629s = x4.02; focused activation pair x4.27); cross-package contention from the 41-package ./... gate modelled explicitly. Cycle-1 claim that both packages share one race factor is WRONG and corrected — atomicity carries 19-20 journal targets vs install s 8, so more of its wall time sits in the race-instrumented O(P^2) namespace validation. Revised projections: install 890-1000s, atomicity 1284-1494s (cycle 1 s 1121s omitted the three post-sweep tests, which sort after the sweep and were never reached). (5) New corrections found this cycle: TestStrictRegistryPolicyFailsUnknown is registry_e2e_test.go:110, the third declaration in that file, so it is test 73 of 107 and 72 completed before the alarm, not 70/71. Cycle 1 s class-independent-injection-cost inference is now MEASURED from an isolated -v atomicity run (logwork RUN-260729-051449:4697-4765, ok 371.708s): wall reconstruction 22.92+301.67+17.89+18.46+9.97=370.91s of 371.708s, project-hybrid-auto chain alone is 81% of package wall, 7 project injections span 34.99-40.20s and 5 global 23.78-31.27s. That yields the separable phases Patch B needs (project baseline+final 40.74s, mean injection 37.28s) and replaces cycle 1 s single-sample 114s/93s. internal/install/atomicity is NOT in candidate-source-delta-post.txt — byte-identical to the accepted tree — so its race overrun is pre-existing debt masked by -timeout 45m, not a jrrgw9 regression. Patch unchanged in shape: (A) t.Parallel on exactly 88 of 107 internal/install tests, 19 excluded by name (11 t.Setenv, 7 afterDocumentOpen hook users, 1 re-exec child), allowlist verified programmatically as exactly {all 107} minus {the 19}; (B) partition the atomicity sweep to one class per scenario with the full class list retained on the post-sweep coverage assertion, 5 distinct global user homes (globalbins.Select prefers each scenario s own userHome/.local/bin, globalbins.go:146-152), plus t.Parallel on the 7 non-sweep top-level tests. Revised projections 220-340s and 340-460s, both bounded by chain length and by 12 performance cores (cycle 1 divided by 16 logical, counting efficiency cores as equals). Corrected cycle-1 overstatement: the pessimistic 2x concurrency case clears the 600s alarm but NOT the 480s pass bar cycle 1 set itself. One deliberate assertion-strength reduction is named for reviewer sanction: partitioning removes the cross-class residue link (21 ordered class pairs today, none after), with the escalation path if it is refused. Out-of-scope production fix re-recorded: saveJournal validates the namespace graph twice per write across 24 call sites, two inside the 32KiB staging copy loop. Outcome resource updated: TASK-260729-3dr6hw_install-race-timeout-diagnosis.md. Logbook entry 2026-07-29 1732.\nHANDOFF NOTE cycle 2 — checklist items 13/14/15 (Implementation matches AC; Solution fits project architecture; Tests green) are deliberately left UNCHECKED and task-board handoff --role researcher was therefore refused (exit non-zero: unchecked checklist items [13 14 15]). Status was set to to-review explicitly instead. Reason, per the Evidence Honesty Contract: this is a read-only diagnosis task whose scope forbids editing the candidate and forbids running the full/race suite, so item 15 has no green command behind it — no Go test, build or vet command was executed in either cycle, and a foreign go test -count=1 ./... (PID 1686) was live throughout cycle 2. Items 13 and 14 concern an implementation this task does not produce and are the reviewer s independent call, not a self-certification. Checking any of the three would be a false green. The three checks belong to the downstream producer task that applies Patch A and Patch B and runs the focused validation commands in section 7 of the diagnosis. Deliverable: .research/260729_install-race-timeouts.md, SHA-256 198e6f83bce44b21a0706c2d7fa74985e9641b6d3f711c39e5167ffebd17b7de, attached as outcome