.task-board/EPIC-260720-21aq1i_declarative-compiled-builds/STORY-260728-2mnlp0_additional-language-driver-protocol/TASK-260728-1g0z69_define-toolchain-requirement-and-guidance-contract/progress.md:138:Reviewer cycle 4 verdict: changes requested and routed to analysis. Evidence: TASK-260728-1g0z69_review-verdict-cycle-4.md. Blocking rework: close the GoVersionRE versus gover.Parse semantic gap exposed by go 1.23.4rc1, and replace the impossible vector-126 equality with separate grammar-completeness and security-forbidden partition properties.
.task-board/EPIC-260720-21aq1i_declarative-compiled-builds/STORY-260728-2mnlp0_additional-language-driver-protocol/TASK-260728-1g0z69_define-toolchain-requirement-and-guidance-contract/progress.md:146:Rework cycle 4 — both cycle-4 blocking findings closed. (1) The go-directive classifier is no longer defined by modfile.GoVersionRE alone. Section 4.2.1 now names the two upstream layers Go composes — modfile shape (GoVersionRE/ToolchainRE) and gover semantics (Parse/FromToolchain) — and defines admitted values as their conjunction at both positions. The layers are incomparable in both directions, so neither alone is the language. go 1.23.4rc1, 1.24.0alpha1 and 1.21.3beta2 are now class 4 metadata_mismatch at Stage B step 5, before cache lookup and before a compiler child; vector 122 replaced, 122a-122d and 124a added, 118 restated as the opposite half of the boundary. Grammars renamed goModVersionLiteral->goModVersionShape and goToolchainVersion->goSemanticVersion. Stated explicitly why this is a classifier case and not the file-shape gate: the file parses and the field extracts, so what rejects it is version semantics, not file syntax; section 5 firing-site list now carries that split. (2) Vector 126 replaced. New section 4.2.1.1 states P1 (C subset of Upstream, no widening) and P2 (Upstream minus F subset of C, no narrowing outside the security partition), giving C = Upstream \ F. C = Upstream is recorded as unsatisfiable rather than strict, because upstream accepts go1.21.0-custom and this contract forbids it; F is neither a subset of nor disjoint from Upstream (go1.23/../evil is forbidden and upstream-rejected), so only C is pinned. F is empty for the go directive, where P2 collapses to equality. Vectors 126/126a-126d cover both properties, the subtraction witness, the F-not-subset witness, and empty F. (3) New section 4.2.1.2 makes the executable probe a normative obligation, implemented and run in .temp/TASK-260728-1g0z69/probe: 13+13 values on Go 1.25.1 and 1.25.5, exit 0, zero divergences, measuring layers independently via go mod edit -json, go mod tidy, and offline GOTOOLCHAIN=local+path. Every upstream fact this cycle was read from this host GOROOT and vendored x/mod or measured by the probe; nothing carried forward from a prior cycle assertion. Delta versus the accepted predecessor is still exactly three files; conformance/v1 and release/1.0.0-rc.5.json byte-identical. Gates standalone with real exit codes: validate 0 (42 schemas, 422 vector files), unittest 0 (29 tests), go test 0, go vet 0, gofmt 0 no output, git diff --check 0 no output, probe gofmt/vet/run 0. Clean probe 02e4ebc: regenerate-check 0 twice, release-check VERSION=1.0.0-rc.5 0. Three expected-red probes reported as failing because they are: injected broken link -> validate exit 1; cycle-3 classification restored -> probe exit 1 with P1 VIOLATED by 1.23.4rc1; C = Upstream forced -> probe exit 1 with security partition subtracts MISMATCH. All reverted, post-revert green. Nothing staged, committed, published, or pinned; no platform claim for rust/swift/kotlin/jdk.
